Month = {JUN 7},
Abstract = {In this research note, candidate survey data from the German
   Longitudinal Election Study (GLES) is used to analyse positional shifts
   of German Bundestag parties between 2013 and 2017. Two developments make
   Germany a particularly interesting case: (1) the liberal but also
   controversial policies of the Merkel cabinet during the European refugee
   crisis and (2) the change of leadership within the right-wing populist
   party Alternative fur Deutschland (AfD). Applying scaling techniques to
   locate candidates of both elections in the same two-dimensional policy
   space, the analysis demonstrates that in 2017 the AfD took a distinct
   radical right position in the party system of Germany. Moreover, the
   study finds that almost all parties moved to the right on the cultural
   left-right dimension in 2017, whereas for the economic left-right
   dimension this has not been the case. Contrary to the mantra of an
   ideological delineation against right-wing populism, there has been a
   robust socio-political conservative shift in the German party system.},
